<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Turkestan ASSR</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1924 CE · capital Tashkent</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first Soviet republic in Central Asia, created from Russian Turkestan and divided into the national republics in 1924.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent-soviet">Tashkent Soviet</a> took power in late 1917 and crushed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kokand-autonomy">Kokand Autonomy</a>, the Turkestan Soviet Republic was proclaimed in April 1918 within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian Soviet Federative Socialist Republic</a>, covering the former <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turkestan-governorate-general">Turkestan Governorate-General</a>. Cut off by civil war fronts until 1919, it was at first dominated by Russian settlers.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkestan-commission">Turkestan Commission</a>, sent in 1919, curbed settler chauvinism and brought Muslim communists into government, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turar-ryskulov">Turar Ryskulov</a>. The republic fought the Basmachi revolts in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-ferghana">Ferghana</a> and famine, and introduced land reform. It governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>, with the Bukharan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khorezm</a> republics as neighbours.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the national delimitation of 1924 the republic was dissolved and its territory split among the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/uzbek-ssr">Uzbek SSR</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turkmen-ssr">Turkmen SSR</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kirghiz-assr">Kara-Kirghiz Autonomous Oblast</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tajik-assr">Tajik ASSR</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kazakh-assr">Kazakh ASSR</a>. This drew the outlines of today's Central Asian states.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/turkestan-assr.jpg" alt="Turkestan ASSR" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: ЦИК ТССР · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turkestan_Autonomous_SSR_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1924 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tashkent</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Turar Ryskulov</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Russian, Uzbek, Kazakh, Turkmen</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, <a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, <a href="/countries/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a>, <a href="/countries/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>, <a href="/countries/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Turkestan Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic, Turkestan Soviet Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1921&amp;focus=turkestan-assr" aria-label="Turkestan ASSR on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6371 704 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Turkestan ASSR lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6691 960L6691 960M6691 960L6741 986M6691 960L6455 1069M6691 960L5820 533M6691 960L6691 960M6691 960L6455 1069"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6691 960h0M6741 986h0M6455 1069h0M5820 533h0M6691 960h0M6455 1069h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6691 960h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6691 960h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tashkent. <a href="/atlas?year=1921&amp;focus=turkestan-assr">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
